            <ArticleTitle>Pachymeningitis with pseudo-Foster Kennedy syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>535-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a case of pachymeningitis with pseudo-Foster Kennedy syndrome in a patient who was positive for perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report. A 44-year-old man was examined for headache and diplopia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Left eye showed limitation of abduction. Ocular fundus, computed tomography (CT), and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the head and orbits were normal. The diplopia subsided spontaneously. Six months later, he noticed sudden visual loss in the left eye. The left eye showed optic disk atrophy and episcleritis, and the right eye showed papilloedema. Computed tomography and MRI exhibited thickened dura mater. Serum perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody level was highly increased.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Pachymeningitis with ocular involvement sometimes requires repeated CT or MRI for diagnosis. This disorder may be caused by microvasculitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
